Great question, Pankaj! If you're using Classic Pipelines in Azure DevOps (i.e. UI-based pipelines, not YAML), here's how you can automate the creation of a new branch after a Pull Request (PR) is merged.


---

✅ Goal: After a PR is merged to main, automatically create and push a new branch with the same file structure.


---

🔧 Step-by-Step in Classic Pipelines

🔹 Step 1: Create a New Classic Build Pipeline

1. Go to Pipelines > Pipelines.


2. Click "New Pipeline" > "Use the Classic Editor".


3. Select your repository (Azure Repos Git).


4. Choose "Start with an empty job".




---

🔹 Step 2: Set the Trigger

1. Go to the Triggers tab.


2. Enable "Enable continuous integration".


3. In branch filters, include only main (or whichever branch you merge PRs into).


4. This ensures the pipeline runs only after a PR is merged into that branch.




---

🔹 Step 3: Add a Script Task to Create the Branch

1. In the pipeline Tasks tab, add a new task:
"Command Line" or "Bash" (if using a Linux agent).


2. Script to use:



git config user.name "azure-pipelines"
git config user.email "azure-pipelines@dev.azure.com"

BRANCH_NAME="post-pr-branch-$(date +%s)"

echo "Creating and pushing branch: $BRANCH_NAME"

git checkout -b $BRANCH_NAME
git push origin $BRANCH_NAME

3. Important: In the "Checkout" step (added by default), make sure:

Clean is checked ✅.

Persist credentials is enabled ✅ (This is what lets it push to the repo).




To check this:

Click on the "Get Sources" step.

Scroll to "Clean" and "Checkout files from the workspace" — enable both.

Enable "Allow scripts to access OAuth token" under "Agent job" → Additional options.



---

🔹 Step 4: Save and Run the Pipeline

Now, when you merge a PR into main, this pipeline will run and create a new branch automatically.


---

✅ Example Result

If a PR is merged at 11:30:12 UTC, the new branch might be:

post-pr-branch-1717506612

You can see it immediately under Repos > Branches.


---

🔐 Permissions Note:

If your repo uses branch protections, make sure your pipeline identity (usually "Project Collection Build Service") has permission to push branches.


